15:32:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes, that absolutely demonstrates the difference—and you’ve just proved the point perfectly. Your old computer’s behavior is exactly what we’d expect, and it reveals a key hardware fact: **the part of a graphics card that plays a movie is not the same part that renders a game.** --- ### A Graphics Card Has Two Very Different Engines 1. **The 3D Rendering Core** (the big, power-hungry part) This is what you think of when you hear “GPU.” Thousands of tiny processors (shader cores) that calculate 3D geometry, textures, lighting, and shaders in real time. Games rely entirely on this. On an old or weak card, this core simply wasn't fast enough to finish the job within 16 milliseconds—so you got lag. 2. **The Video Decode Block** (a small, dedicated, fixed-function circuit) This is a separate, highly specialized piece of silicon specifically designed to decode video compression formats like H.264, MPEG, etc. It is extremely energy-efficient and has been fast enough to play HD movies smoothly for over a decade, even on very low-end integrated graphics or old phones. Playing an animated movie touches *only* this block—not the heavy 3D engine. --- ### Why the Difference Felt So Dramatic on Old Hardware - **Movie playback** is a lightweight, predictable job. The decode block can handle it easily, and the 3D core sits idle. Even a $50 Chromebook from 2013 can play a 1080p Pixar movie without breaking a sweat. - **Game rendering** is a brute-force, real-time calculation. Your old GPU’s 3D core had a tiny fraction of the power of today’s cards. It couldn't keep up with the math, so it lagged. The same card had absolutely no trouble with a movie, because the hard 3D work had already been done years ago in a render farm. **So your experience is not just anecdotal—it’s a direct consequence of the hardware architecture.** The very same physical card can “struggle” and “coast” depending on which internal engine is being used. That’s why you saw such a star